export const body = "<p>Playing a new video game is like learning a new skill. At first, the game will be quite challenging due to your unfamiliarity with the mechanics. But as you progress further and further, you grow more skilled at the game, and the initial difficulty will become more manageable. Therefore, the game will introduce new threats to raise the challenge, keeping you engaged with the gameplay. But manual tuning of difficulty can be off the mark sometimes, leading to difficulty spikes or an overpowered player character. None of which is engaging.</p>\n\n<p>If only there were a way to systemically raise the challenge according to the player's performance... Actually, there is. Such systems are called \"dynamic difficulty balancing.\"</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/c88661be2f288054b28e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/c88661be2f288054b28e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"660\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/0a74752c5fb6206c19e5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/0a74752c5fb6206c19e5.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/92394b2cb35dbb4f2d55.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/92394b2cb35dbb4f2d55.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"645\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Dynamic difficulty is a system that automatically adjusts the difficulty based on the player's performance. The better they do, the harder the game becomes; and vice versa. The goal of this system is to keep the player in the flow state. The goal is to keep the challenge manageable without being tedious.</p>\n\n<p>A famous example of dynamic difficulty is <em>Resident Evil 4</em>. At the \"Normal\" difficulty, which is the standard difficulty of the first playthrough, the game automatically tunes each combat encounter based on how well the player performs. For example, this player was doing very well before they went into this room, and inside he was faced with 9 enemies. After a few deaths, the game lowered the difficulty for him, bringing the total number of enemies down to 7.</p>\n\n<p><em>Left 4 Dead</em> takes this concept one step further with The AI Director. This algorithmic entity handles the enemy count and supply distribution according the players' current situation, status, skill, and location. Specifically, this supervising AI decides how much health, ammo, and weapons are available to the players, along with how frequently special enemies appear.</p>\n\n<p>Other than pure gameplay tuning, The Director also weaves in visual effects, dynamic music, and character communication to create tension. All in all, this system ensures the level of difficulty always rises with the players' skill, making each playthrough fresh regardless of repeating content.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/a5ca61f3186e74e92b96.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/a5ca61f3186e74e92b96.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"718\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/22ad64a181dc3de4d49e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/22ad64a181dc3de4d49e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"645\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/91247af3319ccc6a0b10.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/91247af3319ccc6a0b10.webp\" width=\"969\" height=\"593\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><em>Mario Kart </em>is a spinoff series of racing games that blends speed with action through pick-up items. These games employ a form of dynamic difficulty via their item distribution. Basically, players don't know in advance what items they're going to get, and the leading players will always receive weaker items compared to those racing from behind. This dynamic means, no matter how further ahead you are, you always have to stay alert, and trailing from behind doesn't mean all chances are gone.</p>\n\n<p>On paper, dynamic difficulty seems an ideal choice for every game, but in reality, it isn't. The first reason is it demands a lot of RnD and testing, which adds to the development time and costs. It could result in longer cycles of game releases, which isn't ideal for the current climate of the AAA market.</p>\n\n<p>Second, not every game benefits from dynamic difficulty. <em>Dark Souls</em>, one of the seminal titles of the last decade, didn't even include difficulty selection. The singular difficulty setting allowed the developers to precisely tune every encounter in the game, producing an experience that would otherwise be impossible.</p>\n\n<p>In conclusion, there's no one correct answer for this topic. It just depends on what the developers aim for with their games.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/3bab2b45b92d4069c412.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/3bab2b45b92d4069c412.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>";
